WEBVTT

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.290
You're planning the next shift.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.290
Maintenance can recover extra time at the
mixer.

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:07.110
Is it worth paying for?

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.123
Both products share mixing and packaging.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:14.190
They use different amounts of time at each
step, so extra mixer time helps

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.580
when it lets you run a more valuable mix.

00:00:17.312 --> 00:00:20.292
Compare the best plan you can run now with
the best plan you could run after

00:00:20.292 --> 00:00:21.400
the recovery.

00:00:22.032 --> 00:00:24.362
With these orders, the work pays for
itself.

00:00:24.772 --> 00:00:29.072
The revised plan adds $675 after the
recovery cost.

00:00:29.492 --> 00:00:31.039
Packaging is now full.

00:00:31.452 --> 00:00:34.202
That's the plan we take into the
application and review.

00:00:35.925 --> 00:00:37.262
Now the economics change.

00:00:37.685 --> 00:00:39.745
Product B contributes more per unit.

00:00:40.165 --> 00:00:42.780
The quantities ordered and the time
available stay the same.

00:00:43.205 --> 00:00:44.415
Save that correction.

00:00:44.825 --> 00:00:47.925
The earlier decision becomes stale, while
its figures and review stay

00:00:47.925 --> 00:00:49.955
attached to the evidence used at the time.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.020
Run the comparison again.

00:00:52.514 --> 00:00:55.784
This time, the best choice is to use the
capacity you already have.

00:00:56.114 --> 00:00:59.524
The new mix fills packaging while leaving
time unused at the mixer.

00:00:59.854 --> 00:01:03.444
Recovering more mixing time would produce
the same output and add a cost.

00:01:03.856 --> 00:01:06.184
The changed economics have changed the
decision.

00:01:06.606 --> 00:01:08.336
Both conclusions remain in the history.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.333
You can see what each one relied on.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.940
The earlier review belongs to the earlier
decision, so we record a new

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:14.910
review here.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:20.290
Export the history and open that exact
file in a fresh offline workspace.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.910
It recalculates both decisions from their
original records.

00:01:25.248 --> 00:01:28.078
The next reviewer can examine how each
conclusion was reached.

00:01:29.243 --> 00:01:31.053
Now take a separate maintenance job.

00:01:31.593 --> 00:01:34.533
Before work begins, the planner needs to
know whether a replacement part is

00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:35.363
available.

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:39.465
The i3X demonstration server identifies
Pump 101.

00:01:39.883 --> 00:01:42.393
The task records supply the stock and
reservations.

00:01:42.803 --> 00:01:46.053
All the stock is already reserved, so the
workbench records a hold.

00:01:47.883 --> 00:01:49.573
One reservation was counted twice.

00:01:50.058 --> 00:01:51.808
Correct that record and save the change.

00:01:53.158 --> 00:01:54.108
Run the check again.

00:01:54.518 --> 00:01:57.688
A part is now available, and the history
preserves both earlier holds.

00:01:58.118 --> 00:02:01.158
The equipment response, task records and
review remain separate, so you can

00:02:01.158 --> 00:02:02.588
check where the answer came from.

00:02:04.408 --> 00:02:07.678
Open the handoff in a fresh workspace and
it reproduces both outcomes.

00:02:08.205 --> 00:02:11.145
Across both examples, you can see what
changed, why the decision changed,

00:02:11.145 --> 00:02:13.255
and which decision each review belongs to.
